<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-administration/foundational/the-administrative-state">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Public Administration, and it assumes nothing from earlier
    lessons — only the experience of being governed, which every adult already has. If you have
    queued for a passport, waited on a building permit, or collected a prescription the government
    helps pay for, you have already dealt with the machinery this field studies. Here we give that
    machinery its names, its design and its standing problems.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define the administrative state and explain why democracies — uneasily — delegate power to it;</li>
    <li>describe the classic design of a bureaucracy and the specific promise it was built to keep: impartiality;</li>
    <li>trace how an agency exercises power day to day — writing rules, enforcing them, judging disputes — and how courts and sunshine laws check it;</li>
    <li>read a federal system: which level sets standards, which delivers services, and how grant money steers the whole arrangement;</li>
    <li>judge when government should deliver a service itself and when it should hand it to a firm or a non-profit, and what risks each choice creates.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the whole lesson: whenever an institution appears, we ask what failure
    it was invented to prevent. Institutions are fossilised arguments. To understand one is to
    hear the argument again.
  </p>

  <h2>The gap between promising and delivering</h2>
  <p>
    A parliament can pass a clean-air law in an afternoon. Making the air cleaner takes monitoring
    stations, emission limits, permits for factories, inspectors with test kits, laboratories,
    courts to hear the disputes and budget lines to pay for all of it — sustained for decades. The
    promise is made in politics; the delivery happens somewhere else entirely. That somewhere else
    is the subject of this field.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Public administration</strong> is the study of how government actually runs: the
    agencies, budgets, rules and civil services that turn laws into permits, pensions, inspections
    and pavement. The permanent, professional, unelected part of government is called the
    <strong>administrative state</strong>, and modern government is mostly made of it. In most
    developed countries the civil service employs more people than the armed forces, and
    regulatory agencies shape more of daily life than any legislature ever votes on directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does this need studying? Because it is a standing paradox. Democracy says power belongs to
    the people's elected representatives. But complex, technical, around-the-clock governance needs
    full-time specialists who cannot be elected out of expertise — nobody re-runs a water
    treatment plant on election enthusiasm. Every modern state has answered the paradox the same
    way: delegate enormous power to bureaucracies, then build elaborate machinery to hold them
    accountable. Every serious question in this field is a variation on that theme.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lesson works through three things: the machinery itself (bureaucracy as a design, and how
    agencies wield power), the multi-level geometry of federal systems, and the blurred boundary
    where government hands work to non-profits and private firms.
  </p>

  <h2>Bureaucracy as a design: the promise of impartiality</h2>
  <p>
    The founding problem was named by Woodrow Wilson in "The Study of Administration" (1887): how
    can government be run competently without corrupting democracy, and how can democracy steer it
    without smothering its competence? Wilson's proposed answer, the
    <strong>politics–administration dichotomy</strong>, held that elected politicians should decide
    <em>what</em> to do and neutral professionals should decide <em>how</em> to do it. The dichotomy
    has never survived contact with reality intact — every "how" is also a "what" — but the
    question it framed still organises the field.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="find the faulty line — a dichotomy briefing" widget="spot-error" config={adminDichSpot} />
  <p>
    The American context made the problem urgent. Under the <strong>spoils system</strong>, each
    new administration staffed agencies with political loyalists — the name comes from the saying
    "to the victor belong the spoils" — which produced corruption and incompetence together. The
    assassination of President Garfield in 1881 by a disappointed office-seeker pushed through the
    <strong>Pendleton Act</strong> (1883), which began the merit-based civil service: positions
    filled by examination, protection from political dismissal. Notice what kind of answer that
    is: not a new policy, but a new design for the machine that executes policy.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="scrub the milestones from spoils to sunshine" widget="timeline" config={adminReformTimeline} />
  <p>
    The sociologist Max Weber gave the design its theory. His <strong>"ideal type" of
    bureaucracy</strong> — a deliberately simplified model, the way a physicist uses a
    frictionless plane — has four features: a hierarchy of offices with clear jurisdictions;
    written rules and records ("files"); officials appointed on merit and salaried, not elected or
    purchased; and career ladders that reward expertise. The point of all this is a specific
    promise: <strong>impartiality</strong>. A citizen should get the same permit, the same
    benefit, the same treatment regardless of who they know or what they paid — the rule decides,
    not the official's mood.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why the least glamorous bureaucratic features — the forms, the queues, the documented
    procedures — are best understood as the technology of fairness. A form that treats every
    applicant identically is a promise kept in writing.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "bureaucracy is just waste — a private firm would do the same
  job with half the staff." Some of it is waste, and the complaint has real targets, as we will
  see. But much of what gets called waste is the <em>price of the promise</em>: the duplicate
  signatures and waiting periods exist because a machine built so that no official can quietly
  favour a friend must also be slow when that official wants to help you. Fairness and speed are
  in tension by design, not by accident.</p>

  <h2>The price of the design: red tape and discretion</h2>
  <p>
    The design has known costs, and "bureaucracy" became an insult for a reason. Rules are written
    for the typical case and mishandle the atypical one. Rule-following can displace results — an
    agency can comply perfectly with procedure while the actual problem goes unsolved. Hierarchy
    filters information on the way up and distorts instructions on the way down. The accumulation
    of procedure over purpose earns its nickname, <strong>red tape</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    And here is the deepest complication: discretion never actually disappears. Statutes are
    incomplete — no legislature can foresee every case — so officials must fill gaps. Michael
    Lipsky's study of <strong>street-level bureaucrats</strong> — teachers, police officers,
    caseworkers, inspectors — showed that these frontline workers, facing more clients than time,
    effectively <em>make</em> policy in the patterns of their daily coping: which claims get
    expedited, which infractions ignored, which students pushed. Governance happens at the desk,
    not only in the chamber.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="walk a caseworker's afternoon of discretion" widget="branch" config={adminStreetBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the administrative state exists because delivery needs permanent
    expertise; its design buys impartiality at the price of flexibility; and the discretion
    surviving inside it means the real questions are not "discretion or no discretion" but how
    much, guided by what expertise, under whose oversight. The next two sections take those
    questions in turn.
  </p>

  <h2>How an agency actually wields power</h2>
  <p>
    Where does an agency's power come from? A legislature creates an agency by passing an
    <strong>enabling act</strong> — a statute that works like a job description: it creates the
    agency and states the scope of power delegated to it. Because the main job is enforcing the
    law, most agencies sit in the executive branch, where the president or a governor appoints and
    oversees the leadership. Legislatures also deliberately create <strong>independent
    agencies</strong> — bodies not under the day-to-day direction of the president or governor,
    often run by boards with staggered, cross-party terms — when they want certain decisions
    insulated from politics, so that they survive any single election.
  </p>
  <p>
    Day to day, an agency exercises power in three forms, mirroring the three branches of
    government. <strong>Rulemaking</strong> (quasi-legislative): the agency writes regulations
    that fill in a statute and carry the force of law — the precise emission limit behind a
    clean-air act, for example. <strong>Enforcement</strong> (quasi-executive): investigation and
    inspection; unlike police, agencies generally do not need suspicion of wrongdoing to act — a
    tax office may examine returns at random. <strong>Adjudication</strong> (quasi-judicial):
    trial-like hearings, often before an <strong>administrative law judge</strong>, that decide
    whether a rule was broken and what the penalty is. One institution, three hats.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file each action under its hat" widget="sort-buckets" config={adminHatsSort} />
  <p>
    Legislatures know what they have delegated. In the United States, the Administrative Procedure
    Act standardises how federal agencies must behave — how proposed rules are published and
    opened for public comment, how hearings run — and courts review agency decisions, but only
    within limits: a court checks whether the agency exceeded the authority its enabling act
    granted, misread the law, skipped required procedure, violated the constitution, or reached a
    decision with no basis in facts or law. Judges do not re-decide the policy; expertise gets the
    benefit of the doubt, legality does not.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "agencies cannot make law — only legislatures can." The clean
  version is wrong in an instructive way. Only legislatures can pass statutes, but legislatures
  cannot keep up with the detail modern life needs, so they delegate: agencies produce
  <strong>administrative law</strong> — regulations with real legal force, enforceable in the
  courts, valid only within the scope of the authority the legislature handed over. The chain of
  authority is what keeps this democratic: every agency power traces back to a statute passed by
  an elected body.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="fill the blanks — the delegation's chain of authority" widget="cloze" config={adminLawCloze} />

  <h2>Who watches the watchers: the accountability layers</h2>
  <p>
    Agencies answer to <strong>elected executives</strong> (ministers, presidents, mayors) who
    appoint leadership; to <strong>legislatures</strong> through hearings, budget control and
    questioning; to <strong>courts</strong> through judicial review; and to specialised watchdogs —
    audit offices, inspectors general, ombudsmen — plus the media and interest groups. Sunshine
    laws give citizens a direct tool: the United States' Freedom of Information Act (1966) lets
    any person request government records, and unless one of a short list of exceptions applies
    (national security, personal privacy, trade secrets), the government must disclose.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the span from spoils to sunshine" widget="estimate" config={adminFoiaEst} />
  <p>
    None of these checks is sufficient alone; each catches a different failure. Courts catch
    illegality, auditors catch waste, elections catch unpopularity, the ombudsman catches the
    individual case of unfairness. Layered together they are the legitimacy engine of unelected
    power — the administrative state is defensible exactly to the degree that this layering works.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each watcher with the failure it catches" widget="match" config={adminWatchMatch} />
  <p>
    Reform movements keep rewriting the answer. The mid-century confidence in expert agencies gave
    way in the 1980s and 1990s to the <strong>New Public Management</strong>: import private-sector
    techniques, set targets and performance indicators, contract out delivery, treat citizens as
    customers — the 1992 book <em>Reinventing Government</em> became its manifesto. It bought
    measurable efficiency and paid for it in target-gaming, fragmentation and demoralised
    services. Current waves — co-production, a renewed focus on equity, trust and digital
    government — are corrections in the other direction. Each generation rediscovers Weber's
    trade-off: rules buy fairness and cost flexibility; flexibility buys responsiveness and costs
    consistency. The steps below gather the whole argument in one view.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the machinery and its checks" widget="equationStep" config={adminCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps trace this lesson's spine — what the
    administrative state is, the design and its critique, the discretion that makes oversight
    necessary, the federal geometry that splits the work, and the layered accountability that
    makes delegation defensible. Step 5 is the answer to step 3: oversight exists because
    discretion exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Federalism and multi-level governance: who does what</h2>
  <p>
    Few services citizens actually meet are delivered by a single level of government. A
    <strong>federal system</strong> — one in which authority is constitutionally divided between a
    national government and states or provinces, each with its own legal standing — works as a
    <em>division of labour</em>: the national level sets standards, redistributes money and handles
    what must be uniform (defence, currency, cross-border matters); intermediate governments
    legislate and administer broad domestic programmes; local governments deliver the day-to-day —
    schools, waste, local roads, housing permits — because proximity is itself an administrative
    virtue. The school your child attends is typically staffed and run locally, funded partly by
    the state, regulated and subsidised by the national government. The question "which level is
    responsible?" usually has a three-part answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The plumbing of the arrangement is the <strong>grants system</strong>. National money flows
    down as <strong>categorical grants</strong> (money for a specific purpose, with conditions
    attached) or <strong>block grants</strong> (money for a broad purpose, with more local
    freedom) — the difference is strings, not amounts. Conditions are the national government's
    steering wheel: it cannot always command a state to act, but it can attach requirements to
    money the state wants. This model — shared money, shared administration, negotiated turf — is
    called <strong>cooperative federalism</strong>, and it replaced the older "layer cake" image
    of watertight jurisdictions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why split the work at all? Two principles justify it, and they pull against each other.
    <strong>Subsidiarity</strong>: decisions should be made at the lowest level competent to make
    them, because local knowledge is real knowledge and local accountability is the sharpest.
    <strong>Uniformity</strong>: some goods — rights, environmental floors, a single market — must
    not vary by postcode, so the top level must set the floor. Most federal political fights live
    in the tension between the two. A worked example: clean air. The national agency sets ambient
    standards and emission limits (uniformity); state environmental agencies write the
    implementation plans, issue permits and run inspections (subsidiarity); a municipality runs
    transit and zoning that shape emissions on the ground; and when a state plan falls short, the
    national agency may withhold funding or impose a federal plan. One policy, three levels,
    constant negotiation.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — subsidiarity versus uniformity" widget="compare" config={adminFedCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that federalism means "states do their own thing". In
  practice, most modern federalism is <em>intergovernmental administration</em> — national
  frameworks, subnational implementation, jointly financed — and the daily work of public
  administration happens in these multi-level programmes, not in clean constitutional silos.
  "Local government knows best" and "national standards protect everyone" are both true; the
  craft of the field is managing the trade-off, not picking a side.</p>

  <h2>The non-profit and private interfaces: the blurring state</h2>
  <p>
    The twentieth century ended with government <em>doing less and steering more</em>. Services
    once delivered by public employees are now frequently contracted to private firms (prisons, IT
    systems, waste collection, employment services), delivered by non-profit organisations under
    public funding (social housing associations, charities running employment programmes), or
    produced jointly with citizens. Political scientists call the resulting pattern the
    <strong>hollow state</strong>: the shell of public authority remains — the law, the budget,
    the accountability — while much of the delivery is performed by third parties. Non-profit
    delivery has deep roots; in many countries charities and voluntary hospitals predate the
    welfare state and were folded into public funding rather than replaced.
  </p>
  <p>
    The arguments for the interfaces are real. Competition can lower costs; specialisation brings
    expertise a generalist agency cannot maintain; non-profits reach communities and earn trust in
    ways officials cannot; contracting lets government scale capacity up and down. The arguments
    against are mostly <em>accountability</em> problems in disguise. A private contractor's
    incentive is profit; a non-profit's is mission and survival; neither is identical to the
    public interest, and the gap must be managed by the contract — an instrument that can specify
    what was bought but rarely anticipates what government will need next. Contractors acquire
    expertise government loses ("we cannot replace them because only they understand the system
    they built"). And when an outsourced service fails, citizens blame government anyway, because
    citizens cannot see the corporate shell behind the service.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic research finding here, from Pressman and Wildavsky's study of economic development
    in Oakland, is that implementation fails in the <em>joint</em> action of many independent
    actors, each with veto points and separate priorities — and the failure is most severe when
    the actors are separate organisations in a network rather than units of one hierarchy.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked miniature makes the management task concrete. A city contracts waste collection to a
    private firm and homelessness outreach to a non-profit. The waste contract can be specified —
    bins per street, missed-collection penalties, performance bonds — so competitive tendering
    plausibly works. The outreach contract cannot be specified that way ("trust established"
    resists measurement), so the city relies on relationship management, outcome reporting and the
    non-profit's own accountability to donors and clients. Smart administration therefore matches
    the instrument to the service: contracts for measurable, contestable work; grants and
    partnerships for relational, evolving work; in-house delivery where discretion must remain
    public and expertise must stay internal.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if a service is contracted out, government is no longer
  responsible for it." The contract changes who performs the work, not who answers for it. Voters
  hold the mayor accountable for a failed private prison or a botched IT rollout exactly as if
  the state had done the work in-house — which is precisely why the accountability machinery from
  the earlier sections (audits, performance measures, sunshine laws) has to reach
  <em>through</em> the contract to the third party.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In your own words: what is the administrative state, and what paradox does it create for democracy?</li>
    <li>What specific promise was Weber's bureaucratic design built to keep — and which of its features serve that promise?</li>
    <li>A legislature wants a new food-safety regulator to be insulated from politics. Name one design choice it can make, and one cost of that choice.</li>
    <li>What is the difference between a categorical grant and a block grant — and which gives local officials more discretion?</li>
    <li>A city outsources its bus services to a private company. If the buses stop running, who is accountable — and through what mechanisms?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The permanent, unelected machinery — agencies and civil
    services — that turns laws into delivered services; the paradox is that democratic legitimacy
    rests on elected consent while effective governance needs full-time specialists who cannot be
    voted out of expertise. (2) Impartiality; hierarchy, written rules, merit appointment and the
    career service all serve it by removing personal discretion and favouritism from decisions.
    (3) Make it an independent agency with a staggered, cross-party board — cost: slower response
    to the elected government's agenda. (4) A categorical grant is earmarked for a specific
    purpose with conditions attached; a block grant is for a broad purpose with local freedom —
    the block grant gives local officials more discretion. (5) The government — the mayor and the
    transport department — remains accountable to voters and courts; the mechanisms are the
    contract itself (penalties, termination), performance monitoring and political
    responsibility, which is why oversight must reach through the contractor.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The administrative state is the permanent, professional, unelected machinery that turns laws into delivered services; democracies delegate to it because delivery needs expertise, then build layered accountability to watch it.</li>
    <li>Weber's bureaucratic design — hierarchy, written rules, merit, files — exists to deliver impartiality; its costs are slowness, rule-following over results and red tape.</li>
    <li>Agencies exercise delegated power in three modes — rulemaking, enforcement, adjudication — valid only within the scope of the statute that created them.</li>
    <li>Discretion never disappears: street-level workers make policy in their daily coping, so oversight must be layered — executives, legislatures, courts, auditors, ombudsmen, sunshine laws.</li>
    <li>Federalism is a division of labour: national standards (uniformity), local delivery (subsidiarity), with grant conditions as the national steering wheel.</li>
    <li>Contracting and non-profit delivery create a "hollow state": responsibility stays with government even where performance moves to third parties.</li>
    <li>Match the instrument to the service: contracts for measurable work, partnerships for relational work, in-house delivery where public discretion is essential.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson mapped the machine; the next lessons put it in motion. The policy process — agenda
    setting, formulation, implementation, evaluation — is where the discretion, multi-level
    geometry and third-party networks from this page either cooperate or collide. After that,
    public budgeting shows you the steering wheel itself: how money, the most precise instrument
    of control in government, is raised and spent.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper currents are worth following. First, the <strong>measurement problem</strong>: the
    New Public Management's promise of evidence-based accountability collided with the sociologist's
    law that when a measure becomes a target it ceases to be a good measure — waiting-time targets
    gamed by re-booking, crime statistics massaged, "teaching to the test". The modern synthesis
    treats metrics as one fallible input among several, to be triangulated with professional
    judgement and citizen feedback, rather than as the answer. Second, the <strong>principal–agent
    problem</strong> formalises all delegation: the principal (voters, ministers) cannot perfectly
    observe the agent (agency, contractor), so the design question is incentive and monitoring
    structures under asymmetric information — which is why audits, open data and freedom-of-
    information laws are not bureaucratic decoration but information-forcing devices. Third,
    <strong>trust and capacity</strong>: surveys across the OECD show declining trust in
    government, and the field increasingly argues that service experience is the main driver —
    people judge the state by the queue at the permit office, not by the macroeconomy. That
    reframes competence itself as the foundation of legitimacy: the administrative state is not
    merely machinery that democracy must tolerate, but the thing that makes democratic promises
    physically come true. Its reform debates — how to recruit and keep expertise, how to digitise
    without surveilling, how to buy agility without hollowing out — are, in the end, debates about
    whether modern democracies can still keep their word.
  </p>

